            "text": "E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase which is a component of the N-end rule pathway (By similarity). Recognizes and binds to proteins bearing specific N-terminal residues that are destabilizing according to the N-end rule, leading to their ubiquitination and subsequent degradation (By similarity). In complex with ced-3, required for the ced-3-mediated cleavage and subsequent degradation of the heterochronic protein lin-28 to regulate seam cell fate patterning during larval development (PubMed:28602583). Negatively regulates glutamate metabolism through the aspartate aminotransferase got-1.2 (PubMed:29649217). Modulation of glutamate levels most likely controls locomotory behavior, in particular backwards locomotion or 'reversals' (PubMed:29649217).",
            "evidence_refs": [],
            "xml": "<ns0:comment xmlns:ns0=\"https://uniprot.org/uniprot\" type=\"function\">\n        <ns0:text evidence=\"1 5 6\">E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase which is a component of the N-end rule pathway (By similarity). Recognizes and binds to proteins bearing specific N-terminal residues that are destabilizing according to the N-end rule, leading to their ubiquitination and subsequent degradation (By similarity). In complex with ced-3, required for the ced-3-mediated cleavage and subsequent degradation of the heterochronic protein lin-28 to regulate seam cell fate patterning during larval development (PubMed:28602583). Negatively regulates glutamate metabolism through the aspartate aminotransferase got-1.2 (PubMed:29649217). Modulation of glutamate levels most likely controls locomotory behavior, in particular backwards locomotion or 'reversals' (PubMed:29649217).</ns0:text>\n    </ns0:comment>\n    "
